# Line endings, pinned.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout rewrites every text file to CRLF on the way
# out of git and back to LF on the way in. That is invisible in a diff and harmless right
# up until something reads a file as TEXT rather than as lines — and this project does
# exactly that in two places:
#
#   * `prediction/divergence.test.js` strips comments out of `divergence.js` and asserts
#     the remaining source contains no `|| 0`, `??`, `Number(`, `parseFloat(` or
#     `parseInt(`. Its stripper is `line.replace(/\/\/.*$/, "")`, and in JavaScript `.`
#     does not match `\r` while `$` without the `m` flag means end-of-string. On a CRLF
#     tree every line ends `\r`, so NOT ONE comment was stripped, the header — which
#     discusses all five idioms by name — was scanned as if it were code, and the test
#     failed on this machine while passing on every LF checkout including CI.
#
#   * `prediction/schema-contract.test.js` parses `schema.prisma` as text for the same
#     class of reason.
#
# A test that is red locally and green on CI is worse than one that is simply red: it
# teaches whoever sees it that local failures do not mean anything. 149 files under
# server/src and client/src were CRLF in the working tree when this was written.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to normalise to LF in the repository AND to check out LF
# everywhere, on every platform, regardless of what core.autocrlf says. The tooling here
# is Node, Prisma and Vite; none of them wants CRLF, and nothing in this repo is edited by
# a Windows tool that requires it.
#
# AFTER ADDING THIS, the working tree must be refreshed once — the files are already
# LF in the index, so this only rewrites what is checked out:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git status          # expect no changes: the index was LF all along
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, if any are ever added, genuinely do want CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary. Marking these explicitly stops git guessing, which it gets wrong often enough
# to corrupt an icon or a PDF on checkout.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
*.p12 binary
